This is a strong specification, but it is not yet a production-ready EA design. The main strengths are that it combines pattern detection, confirmation, and risk control, which matches the source material’s emphasis on neckline breaks, candle-close confirmation, and avoiding premature entries.
What’s good
It correctly centers the EA on the two most tradeable families in the framework: reversal patterns and continuation patterns.
The “wait for close” rule is especially important because the source explicitly warns against entering on mere completion or touch of the pattern.
The added candlestick filter is sensible because the source recommends confirmation from candles like Hammer, Engulfing, and Pin Top after breakout.
The staged exit logic is more advanced than the source, but it is directionally consistent with the idea of capturing the next swing move after the initial measured move.
What needs tightening
The pattern universe is too broad for phase one. A fully autonomous detector for Head and Shoulders, Double Tops/Bottoms, Triple Tops/Bottoms, Rounded Tops/Bottoms, Triangles, and Pennants will be fragile unless you define swing-point rules, minimum spacing, tolerance bands, and invalidation rules very explicitly.
“Dynamic neckline” needs mathematical rules, not just intent. You should define whether the neckline is horizontal, least-squares fitted, or anchored to specific swing lows/highs, because that affects signal quality and backtest consistency.
The partial-close and trailing-stop logic should be separated from pattern recognition as its own position-management module. That keeps the EA easier to debug and lets you swap exit logic without changing detection logic.
The kill switch needs a precise equity-drawdown definition, such as peak equity-to-current equity, and whether it is per-symbol, per-account, or per-session.
Best build order
Double Top / Double Bottom module.
Head and Shoulders / Inverted Head and Shoulders module.
Continuation patterns: triangles and pennants.
Candlestick confirmation filter.
Risk manager with SL, partial close, break-even, and trailing stop.
Global kill switch and multi-timeframe orchestration.
That order is practical because Double Top/Bottom is the cleanest pattern family to formalize first, and the source itself treats it as a core reversal structure with explicit neckline logic and clear stop placement.
My recommendation
Start with Double Top / Double Bottom first, not Head and Shoulders. It is simpler to define mathematically, easier to backtest, and will give you a reliable template for swing detection, neckline validation, and close-beyond-breakout logic before you move to the more ambiguous three-peak structures.
A good implementation spec for phase one would be:
swing detection,
peak/trough similarity tolerance,
neckline construction,
candle-close breakout,
confirmation candle scoring,
SL at invalidation swing,
1:2 partial exit,
BE plus trailing remainder.
That gives you a clean core architecture before expanding to the full 28-pattern universe.
